'''Doble8, S.A.''' (stylised as '''88''') is the largest {{w|convenience store}} chain in southern [[Abyala]], operating approximately 1,700 stores across [[Balboa]], [[Sinchew]], [[Tapuya]], and [[Lacashe]]. The company is headquartered in the [[Districte Financer, Sant Cristòfor|Districte Financer]], [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]], and is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker DBL8.

The chain was founded in 1996 by [[Rafael Vidal i Comas]], a mixed-race Balboan entrepreneur who grew up in a [[Sinchew]]ese community in Sant Cristòfor. The company's name derives from the number 8, which is considered a symbol of prosperity and good fortune in Sinchewese culture. Vidal i Comas opened his first store on 8 August 1996 and adopted the stylised "88" as the company's brand identity, drawing on the cultural influence of the community in which he was raised.

== History ==

=== Founding (1996) ===

Rafael Vidal i Comas was born and raised in Sant Cristòfor in a mixed-race Balboan family. His neighbourhood had a large Sinchewese community, and from an early age he was immersed in Sinchewese cultural traditions, including the belief that the number 8 brings prosperity and good fortune. In Sinchewese culture, the number 8 is associated with wealth and success.

Vidal i Comas worked in retail from his teenage years, progressing from stock clerk to store manager. In the mid-1990s, he identified a gap in the Balboan market for a modern convenience store format, modelled on the small-format retail chains that had emerged in Okaiken and other developed markets but adapted to the Abyalan context. He pooled his savings with a small loan and opened his first store in Sant Cristòfor on 8 August 1996 — a date chosen for its double 8s. He named the store "88" (Doble8), drawing on the Sinchewese cultural symbol of good fortune that had been a part of his upbringing.

The first store was a success, offering extended hours, a curated selection of snacks, beverages, basic groceries, and prepared food, and a clean, modern retail format that was uncommon in Balboa at the time. Within the first year, Vidal i Comas opened three additional locations in Sant Cristòfor.

=== Rapid expansion (1997–2010) ===

Doble8 expanded rapidly across Balboa in the late 1990s and early 2000s, reaching 100 stores by 2001 and 300 by 2005. The company's growth was driven by a combination of company-owned stores and a {{w|franchising|franchise model}} that allowed local entrepreneurs to operate Doble8-branded stores under the company's supply chain and operational standards.

In 2003, Doble8 expanded beyond Balboa for the first time, entering the [[Sinchew]]ese market — a natural extension given the cultural connection between the company's brand and Sinchewese traditions. The Sinchewese market proved highly receptive to the brand, and Sinchew became Doble8's largest market by store count within five years.

The company expanded into [[Tapuya]] in 2006 and [[Lacashe]] in 2009, completing its footprint across southern Abyala. By 2010, Doble8 operated approximately 900 stores across its four markets.

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 2004, raising capital to fund its international expansion. The Vidal family retained a 28 per cent stake, with the remainder held by public investors.

=== Modern era (2010–present) ===

In the 2010s, Doble8 continued to expand its store network, reaching 1,500 stores by 2018 and approximately 1,700 by 2026. The company invested in modernising its stores, introducing digital payment systems, {{w|self-checkout}} kiosks, and a mobile app for loyalty rewards and mobile payments.

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 had a mixed impact on Doble8. While foot traffic declined during lockdowns, the company's convenience format — focused on quick, essential purchases — proved more resilient than larger retail formats. The company also expanded its delivery and {{w|click and collect}} services in response to the pandemic.

By 2026, Doble8 operated approximately 1,700 stores across its four markets, employed approximately 18,000 people, and generated annual revenue of $4.2 billion. The company's {{w|market capitalization}} of $5.0 billion made it one of the larger retail companies listed on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor.

== Operations ==

Doble8 operates approximately 1,700 stores across four markets:

* [[Sinchew]]: approximately 850 stores — the company's largest market by store count, reflecting the cultural resonance of the "88" brand in Sinchewese culture
* [[Balboa]]: approximately 380 stores — concentrated in the Sant Cristòfor metropolitan area and the Estret corridor, with additional stores in Sant Bart and other interior cities
* [[Tapuya]]: approximately 320 stores
* [[Lacashe]]: approximately 180 stores

The company operates a mixed model of company-owned stores and franchised locations. Approximately 60 per cent of stores are company-owned, while 40 per cent are operated by franchisees under the Doble8 brand, supply chain, and operational standards.

Doble8 stores typically offer a range of convenience products, including snacks, beverages (including [[Coccola]] and other HAWT Inc.-branded products), basic groceries, prepared food, tobacco, personal care items, prepaid mobile services, and {{w|ATM}} access. Many stores operate 24 hours or extended hours.

== Ownership ==

Doble8's ownership structure reflects its origins as a founder-led business and its status as a publicly traded company:

* [[Vidal family]]: 28 per cent — the founding family, descended from Rafael Vidal i Comas
* Public float: 72 per cent — traded on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor

Rafael Vidal i Comas remains chairman of the company. The CEO position is held by a professional manager, with day-to-day operations managed by an executive team drawn from the retail and convenience store industry.

